%description:

Tcp.accEcnOptionEnabled:changed -- a new value acts at the next open; the open connection keeps the option.

The claim (Tcp.ned): "AccECN: send the AccECN option, which gives the byte counts of each ECN
codepoint (RFC 9768 section 3.2.3.3: RECOMMENDED); false: only the ACE field (Linux
tcp_ecn_option=0). Each connection reads it at its open".

The classes: on (true, the default), off (false), and changed (the value changes during the
run; the parameter is @mutable). The code: TcpConnection::configureStateVariables() reads it at
the open; the AccECN option goes on the SYN-ACK, on the ACK of the handshake and on the first
data segment of the client, and later after a byte count changed (TcpConnection::writeHeaderOptions()). This test separates "changed" from "off".

Host under test: host1, an AccECN client (tcpEcnMode "accecn"; host2 "accecn-passive":
preconditions). A header of 32 bytes has the Timestamps option only; the AccECN option adds 12
bytes. host1 sends the data of its first connection at 0.6 s. At 0.5 s, the test sets
accEcnOptionEnabled of host1 to false, and host1 opens a second connection at 1 s.
Observation: level 1, the ACK of the first handshake and the first data segment have the
option; the ACK of the second handshake has none.

%# parameter: Tcp.accEcnOptionEnabled
%# class: changed
%# value: true
%# key: *.host1.tcp.accEcnOptionEnabled
%# expected-result: PASS

%includes:
#include "ProtocolTest.h"

%global:

using namespace inet;
using namespace inet::protocoltest;

Define_ProtocolTestProgram()
{
    return ProtocolTest("TcpAccEcnOptionEnabledChanged")
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && tcp.ackBit == true && pk.dataLength <= tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.headerLength > 32B")
             .describe("the ACK of the first handshake")
             .within(0.5))
        .act(action().at(0.5).setParameter("host1.tcp", "accEcnOptionEnabled", "false")
                 .describe("accEcnOptionEnabled of host1 becomes false"))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("pk.dataLength > tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.headerLength > 32B")
             .describe("the first data segment of the first connection")
             .within(0.3))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == true && tcp.ackBit == false")
             .describe("the SYN of the second connection")
             .within(1.0))
        .once(on("host1.tcp").signal("packetSentToLower")
             .filterPacket("tcp.synBit == false && tcp.ackBit == true && pk.dataLength <= tcp.headerLength")
             .first()
                  .assertPacket("tcp.headerLength == 32B")
             .describe("the ACK of the second handshake")
             .within(0.1));
}

%activity:
EV << "TcpAccEcnOptionEnabledChanged compiled\n";

%inifile: omnetpp.ini

[General]
*.host1.tcp.accEcnOptionEnabled = true    # the parameter under test
*.host1.tcp.tcpEcnMode = "accecn"
*.host2.tcp.tcpEcnMode = "accecn-passive"
*.host1.app[0].tSend = 0.6s
*.host1.numApps = 2
*.host1.app[1].typename = "TcpSessionApp"
*.host1.app[1].connectAddress = "host2"
*.host1.app[1].connectPort = 1000
*.host1.app[1].tOpen = 1.0s
*.host1.app[1].tSend = 1.1s
*.host1.app[1].sendBytes = 1000B
*.host1.app[1].tClose = 1000s
include ../../parameter/ini/_bulk.ini
include ../../parameter/ini/_base.ini

%contains: stdout
PROTOCOLTEST TcpAccEcnOptionEnabledChanged: PASS
